title:   The keyboard, the screen and supervisor mode
level:   after the first program
needs:   assembler, emulator, rom
learn:   why the hardware is off limits in user mode, and what Super(0) buys you
source:  template "Keyboard/input skeleton" as keyboard.s

step  Assemble and run it
  teach  This template reads a key at a time with GEMDOS Cconin and loops until
         ESC. {key:run} boots it and stops at the entry point, the same way the
         first lesson did.
  do     build
  expect problems == none

step  Read keys
  teach  Cconin is GEMDOS trap #1, function 1: it waits for a key, echoes it to
         the screen the way a TOS prompt would, and leaves it in D0. That is all
         this program does, and it is all a *user-mode* program can do with the
         keyboard.
  do     run
  expect stopped
  do     explain Cconin

step  Why the next program needs something this one does not
  teach  The ST's hardware registers are not in user space. Writing $ffff8240 —
         palette entry zero — from user mode does not set a colour; it bus-errors
         and takes the program down. demo/keyred.s is the same idea one step
         further: it calls GEMDOS Super(0) to enter supervisor mode, sets the
         palette with XBIOS Setcolor, and beeps the PSG, and every one of those
         needs the supervisor bit. If a hardware write dies on you, that is the
         first thing to check, and the reference below has the call you need.
  nominal the check is the next program's, not this one's
  do     explain Setcolor
